1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,144
How do you stop someone testing stolen cards on a payment page?

2
00:00:03,194 --> 00:00:08,472
In Lesson 11, the card frame let a buyer type a card on any shop's payment page.

3
00:00:08,522 --> 00:00:14,880
In Lesson 9, each card got a fingerprint, and in Lesson 10, its first digits named its network.

4
00:00:14,930 --> 00:00:20,856
This lesson uses that fingerprint and those digits to stop a thief testing stolen cards.

5
00:00:20,906 --> 00:00:23,088
Picture a thief with a bag of keys.

6
00:00:23,138 --> 00:00:26,976
They try key after key in a shop's lock, to find the ones that fit.

7
00:00:27,026 --> 00:00:30,480
Locking that door after three wrong keys does not help.

8
00:00:30,530 --> 00:00:32,352
They just walk to the next door.

9
00:00:32,402 --> 00:00:34,992
Here, the keys are stolen card numbers.

10
00:00:35,042 --> 00:00:36,936
The doors are payment sessions.

11
00:00:36,986 --> 00:00:39,912
A thief buys ten thousand stolen numbers.

12
00:00:39,962 --> 00:00:41,784
Most of them are dead.

13
00:00:41,834 --> 00:00:45,480
To find the live ones, they try them on a small shop's payment page.

14
00:00:45,530 --> 00:00:47,352
That is called card testing.

15
00:00:47,402 --> 00:00:49,512
Every try reaches a card network.

16
00:00:49,562 --> 00:00:52,656
Every try may text a code to a real person's phone.

17
00:00:52,706 --> 00:00:59,808
So card testing costs the bank money, floods strangers with codes, and hands the thief a list of working cards.

18
00:00:59,858 --> 00:01:03,168
Each session already allows only a few tries.

19
00:01:03,218 --> 00:01:05,400
When they run out, the session ends.

20
00:01:05,450 --> 00:01:08,064
But the thief just opens a new session.

21
00:01:08,114 --> 00:01:10,776
So counting per session is not enough.

22
00:01:10,826 --> 00:01:13,848
The card zone counts the keys, not the doors.

23
00:01:13,898 --> 00:01:17,880
It writes every attempt down before any network is asked.

24
00:01:17,930 --> 00:01:22,896
Attempts made at the same moment count each other, so speed does not help the thief.

25
00:01:22,946 --> 00:01:25,560
Each attempt is kept by four things.

26
00:01:25,610 --> 00:01:28,344
The card, by its fingerprint, never its number.

27
00:01:28,394 --> 00:01:30,216
The card's first digits.

28
00:01:30,266 --> 00:01:32,280
The buyer's address on the internet.

29
00:01:32,330 --> 00:01:34,152
And the shop.

30
00:01:34,202 --> 00:01:38,016
The bank chooses a limit for each of the four, and a time window.

31
00:01:38,066 --> 00:01:43,944
So many failed tries per card, per first digits, per address and per shop, within that window.

32
00:01:43,994 --> 00:01:47,856
These are the bank's chosen limits and the bank's chosen window.

33
00:01:47,906 --> 00:01:51,792
A limit on tries within a time window is called a velocity limit.

34
00:01:51,842 --> 00:01:57,336
Past any one of the four limits, a new try is refused before any network is asked.

35
00:01:57,386 --> 00:01:59,208
No code is sent.

36
00:01:59,258 --> 00:02:01,080
No attempt is spent.

37
00:02:01,130 --> 00:02:04,752
And no new session opens for that shop until the window passes.

38
00:02:04,802 --> 00:02:08,208
Only failures that say something about the card count against it.

39
00:02:08,258 --> 00:02:10,080
A wrong code counts.

40
00:02:10,130 --> 00:02:12,864
A card the buyer's bank refuses counts.

41
00:02:12,914 --> 00:02:16,968
A mistyped expiry date does not hold back a whole group of cards.

42
00:02:17,018 --> 00:02:22,656
And the card zone's own failures, such as a network being down, never count against anyone.

43
00:02:22,706 --> 00:02:26,088
When refusals pile up, an alert goes out to the team.

44
00:02:26,138 --> 00:02:27,960
Now watch the product.

45
00:02:28,010 --> 00:02:29,832
A buyer types the wrong code.

46
00:02:29,882 --> 00:02:33,336
The form says the code is wrong, and how many tries are left.

47
00:02:33,386 --> 00:02:35,304
The buyer runs out of tries.

48
00:02:35,354 --> 00:02:37,176
The form clears.

49
00:02:37,226 --> 00:02:44,136
The payment page shows a yellow note: that attempt ended, too many tries or the time ran out, enter the card again.

50
00:02:44,186 --> 00:02:46,584
A fresh, empty form appears.

51
00:02:46,634 --> 00:02:48,816
That is a normal buyer having a bad day.

52
00:02:48,866 --> 00:02:52,512
Now the thief, with a script, fires card after card.

53
00:02:52,562 --> 00:02:55,320
For a while, each card is just refused.

54
00:02:55,370 --> 00:02:57,288
Then a counter reaches its limit.

55
00:02:57,338 --> 00:03:03,912
From that moment, the form answers every new card the same way: "Too many unsuccessful attempts.

56
00:03:03,962 --> 00:03:06,816
Try again later." No code is sent.

57
00:03:06,866 --> 00:03:08,688
No network is asked.

58
00:03:08,738 --> 00:03:10,584
No stranger's phone buzzes.

59
00:03:10,634 --> 00:03:13,632
On the bank's side, an alert email arrives.

60
00:03:13,682 --> 00:03:16,920
It says card entry was refused by a velocity rule.

61
00:03:16,970 --> 00:03:23,760
It names the caller, and the counter that tripped: the card, the first digits, the address, or the shop.

62
00:03:23,810 --> 00:03:29,016
It tells the team where to look next: the logs, and the buyer addresses behind the refusals.

63
00:03:29,066 --> 00:03:30,888
What does this protect?

64
00:03:30,938 --> 00:03:35,088
It protects cardholders, whose phones would otherwise fill with codes.

65
00:03:35,138 --> 00:03:39,864
It protects the shop, whose page would otherwise become a thief's testing bench.

66
00:03:39,914 --> 00:03:45,816
And it protects the bank, which would otherwise pay for every try and hand thieves a list of live cards.

67
00:03:45,866 --> 00:03:47,688
Back to the bag of keys.

68
00:03:47,738 --> 00:03:50,832
Every door now counts the same keys.

69
00:03:50,882 --> 00:03:53,184
Count the keys, not the doors.

70
00:03:53,234 --> 00:03:56,016
Next: how all these layers work together.
